                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="944-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_xy.png" width=27 height=27 alt="X/Y Stereo" title="X/Y Stereo" ><img id="944-1" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_ms.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Mid-Side Stereo" title="Mid-Side Stereo" ></span>Audio-Technica AT4050ST</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Multi-Pattern Condenser Stereo Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The AT4050ST is a stereo version of the company&#8217;s multipattern FET LDC, the AT4050. It contains two dual-diaphragm capsules; the front-facing capsule is fixed for Cardioid pickup, while the side-facing capsule is fixed for Figure-8. The output of these two capsules is internally matrixed into three possible stereo formats:</p><ul><li>Mid-side</li><li>90&deg; (XY) stereo</li><li>127&deg; stereo</li></ul><p><a rel="shadowbox" href="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images//mic_extras/audio-technica/AT4050ST-circuit.jpg"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/audio-technica/AT4050ST-circuit-sm.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="AT4050ST Circuit" width="133" /></a>We believe the capsules are identical to those in the AT4050: 2-micron, 21.4mm diameter, externally-polarized, large-diaphragm, center-terminated designs covered with a nickel-plated brass baffle. In other mics this functions as a resonator plate, often to enhance the mic&#8217;s HF response; we speculate that this baffle has a similar effect here.</p><p>Switches on the mic body enable an optional pad (10dB), low-frequency rolloff (-12dB/octave @ 80Hz), and control the stereo output as noted above.</p><p>Sensitivity and self-noise figures vary with output pattern:</p><table><tr><td></td><td><b>Sensitivity</b></td><td><b>Self-noise</b></td></tr><tr><td>Mid-side</td><td>15.8 mV/Pa</td><td>15dBA mid, 17dBA side</td></tr><tr><td>90&deg; Stereo</td><td>7.9 mV/Pa</td><td>25 dBA</td></tr><tr><td>127&deg; Stereo</td><td>8.9 mV/Pa</td><td>16 dBA</td></tr></table><p>The mic&#8217;s headbasket is described as an &#8220;open acoustical environment&#8221; designed to &#8220;minimize unwanted internal reflections.&#8221;</p><p>The mic ships with a low-profile elastic shockmount (Audio-Technica p/n AT8449), a &#8220;dust cover,&#8221; a 5-meter breakout cable (XLR-5F to 2xXLR-3M), and a padded vinyl case (p/n R1).</p><p>The AT4050 was first introduced at the AES-NYC show in the Fall of 2009.</p><blockquote><p><cite>Recording Magazine, 2012</cite><p>For the most part, this mic sounds exactly like a phase-accurate pair of classic AT4050s.</p></p></blockquote><p><p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Audio-Technica/AT4050ST">Audio-Technica AT4050ST</a></p><p>The mic was released in 2009.</p>
